cff-version: 1.2.0
message: 'If you use lexsync, please cite the software. A manuscript describing it is in preparation.'
title: 'lexsync: Lexical optimisation and hardware-timed experiment generation'
abstract: >-
  lexsync is a cross-platform, dual-language (R and Python) toolkit that unifies
  lexical-corpus access (bundled English, Spanish and Mandarin lexica and a
  registry of citable corpora), parallel multidimensional stimulus matching,
  counterbalancing, item resampling, deterministic pseudoword
  generation and the automated generation of hardware-timed PsychoPy,
  OpenSesame and jsPsych experiment scripts for EEG/ERP and browser-based
  research. The MIT licence covers the source code; the bundled example lexica
  are derived from wordfreq (Speer, 2022) and are distributed under
  CC BY-SA 4.0, with the terms and the attribution they require in LICENSE-DATA
  and corpora/ATTRIBUTION.md.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Bernabeu
    given-names: Pablo
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1083-2460'
version: 0.1.1
# This file describes the repository, which holds both packages, so its DOI is
# Zenodo's concept DOI, which archives every GitHub release of both and favours
# neither. Each package's own citation gives that package's identifier: the R
# package's gives the DOI CRAN assigned to it, and the Python package's gives
# this one, since PyPI assigns no DOI.
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22906962
# The packages are released in lockstep. This is the day 0.1.1 first became
# public, on CRAN. PyPI published it on 2026-09-26.
date-released: '2026-09-25'
# Each identifier, marked with the package it names. CFF's repository-artifact
# holds a single address, which would favour one distribution, so CRAN and PyPI
# are both listed here instead.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22906962
    description: "Both packages: every GitHub release of the repository, archived on Zenodo. The Python package's citation gives this DOI."
  - type: doi
    value: 10.32614/CRAN.package.lexsync
    description: "The R package: the DOI CRAN assigned to it, across all its versions. The R package's citation gives this DOI."
  - type: url
    value: 'https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=lexsync'
    description: The R package on CRAN.
  - type: url
    value: 'https://pypi.org/project/lexsync/'
    description: The Python package on PyPI.
# Two licences, because the release ships both: MIT for the code, CC BY-SA 4.0 for
# the wordfreq-derived example lexica inside each package.
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-SA-4.0
repository-code: 'https://github.com/pablobernabeu/lexsync'
keywords:
  - psycholinguistics
  - lexical statistics
  - stimulus matching
  - counterbalancing
  - pseudoword generation
  - EEG
  - ERP
  - PsychoPy
  - OpenSesame
  - jsPsych
  - FAIR
# No preferred-citation until the manuscript describing lexsync is accepted.
# GitHub's 'Cite this repository' button renders preferred-citation in place of
# the software record, so an entry for the unpublished manuscript sent readers to
# a work they could not read, and left out the DOI above.
